# Shared actions (`S-3e`, decision 24): an order and a cart line, each given back as the id the next
# step needs. Like `catalog.tflw`'s `create product`, the bearer token is a parameter — an action
# has no closure over its caller (SPEC §8) — so a test says whose order it is at the call.
#
# Each replaces a four-line block several journeys repeated verbatim. What they assert (201, the id
# captured) is the same assertion those journeys made; the journey's own `expect`s about the order
# or the cart stay in the journey.

action place order(productId, quantity, token)
  api POST /orders body { items: [{ productId: {productId}, quantity: {quantity} }] }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {token}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as orderId
  give orderId

action add to cart(productId, quantity, token)
  api POST /cart/items body { productId: {productId}, quantity: {quantity} }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {token}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as itemId
  give itemId
